<section>
  <div class="container narrow">
    <h2>What this program is</h2>
    <p>This is a guided journey, not a diet. Over several weeks, you learn how Type 2 Diabetes develops in the body, what makes it worsen, and which everyday choices reverse it. You eat real food, cooked at home, with growing skill and confidence. A doctor and nutritionist are with you the whole way.</p>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <ul class="checks">
      <li>Adults living with Type 2 Diabetes — newly diagnosed or long-standing.</li>
      <li>Pre-diabetics with rising HbA1c, fasting sugar or insulin resistance.</li>
      <li>Family members of people with diabetes who want to prevent it.</li>
      <li>Anyone whose doctor has flagged metabolic health concerns.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What you can expect</h2>
    <ul class="checks">
      <li>Daily lessons explaining the why behind each food choice.</li>
      <li>Plant-based, whole-food meal plans built for Indian kitchens.</li>
      <li>Group calls with a doctor and nutritionist, plus written guidance.</li>
      <li>Tracking of fasting glucose, post-meal glucose and HbA1c trends.</li>
      <li>An alumni community to keep your habits alive after the program ends.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="quote">
      <p>"I came in on three medications. By the end of the program, on advice from my own doctor, I was off two of them — and my fasting sugar was the lowest it had been in a decade."</p>
      <cite>— Diabetes Reversal alum (illustrative)</cite>
    </div>

    <h2>How to start</h2>
    <p>The next intake date and fees are listed on the registration page. If you want to discuss your situation before signing up, write to us — a team member will respond within two working days.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a href="contact.html#enrol" class="btn btn-primary">Enquire about the next batch</a>
      <a href="programs.html" class="btn btn-outline">All programs</a>
    </div>

    <p class="notice mt-4">Important: this program complements — it does not replace — your relationship with your doctor. Any change in medication should be made only on your doctor's advice, based on your own clinical readings.</p>
  </div>
</section>
